---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DaysUntilChristmas from '@/components/tools/datetime/DaysUntilChristmas.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="days-until-christmas">
  <DaysUntilChristmas client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the countdown works</h2>
    <p>
      The Christmas clock at the top counts down to midnight at the start of 25 December in the timezone you
      select. It calculates from your device clock on each tick, so it stays exact even after hours in a
      background tab. Below it, each tracked holiday shows the number of whole calendar days remaining, counted
      from today where you are.
    </p>
    <p>
      The page has no stored lookup table. Each date comes from the rule that defines the holiday, calculated when
      the page loads. For this reason, the list updates automatically the day after each holiday, and the six-year
      table works for any span.
    </p>

    <h2>Fixed-date holidays</h2>
    <p>
      These are on the same calendar date each year, but the weekday changes. The Gregorian year is 365.2425 days
      long, so a fixed date moves forward one weekday each year, and two across a leap year.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Holiday</th>
            <th>Date</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>New Year's Day</td><td>1 January</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Valentine's Day</td><td>14 February</td></tr>
          <tr><td>St Patrick's Day</td><td>17 March</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Juneteenth</td><td>19 June</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Independence Day (US)</td><td>4 July</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Halloween</td><td>31 October</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Veterans Day</td><td>11 November</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Christmas Eve</td><td>24 December</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Christmas Day</td><td>25 December</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Boxing Day</td><td>26 December</td></tr>
          <tr><td>New Year's Eve</td><td>31 December</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Holidays defined by a weekday rule</h2>
    <p>
      These move within a fixed window each year. The page stores the rule, not the date.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Holiday</th>
            <th>Rule</th>
            <th>Possible dates</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Martin Luther King Jr. Day</td><td>3rd Monday of January</td><td>15–21 January</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Presidents' Day</td><td>3rd Monday of February</td><td>15–21 February</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mother's Day (US)</td><td>2nd Sunday of May</td><td>8–14 May</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Memorial Day</td><td>Last Monday of May</td><td>25–31 May</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Father's Day (US)</td><td>3rd Sunday of June</td><td>15–21 June</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Labor Day (US)</td><td>1st Monday of September</td><td>1–7 September</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Thanksgiving (US)</td><td>4th Thursday of November</td><td>22–28 November</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Black Friday</td><td>Day after Thanksgiving</td><td>23–29 November</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cyber Monday</td><td>Monday after Thanksgiving</td><td>26 November to 2 December</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Easter and the computus</h2>
    <p>
      Easter is the hardest date in the western calendar to calculate. The rule agreed at the Council of Nicaea in
      325 puts Easter Sunday on the first Sunday after the first ecclesiastical full moon on or after 21 March.
      "Ecclesiastical" is important: the church uses a tabular approximation of the lunar cycle, not the real moon,
      so Easter can differ from the astronomical answer by a week.
    </p>
    <p>
      This page uses the anonymous Gregorian algorithm, usually credited to Meeus, Jones and Butcher, which
      calculates the date for any Gregorian year. Easter falls between 22 March and 25 April, a 35-day window, and
      Good Friday, Ash Wednesday, Ascension and Pentecost move with it. Eastern Orthodox churches apply the same
      rule to the Julian calendar, so their Easter is usually on a different Sunday.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the day count can differ by one</h2>
    <p>
      "Days until" is a count of calendar days, not elapsed hours divided by 24. At 23:00 on 20 December where you
      are, Christmas is 5 days away, although only about 4 days and 1 hour of real time remain. People usually mean
      this answer. For this reason the reference timezone matters near midnight: it is already tomorrow in Auckland
      while it is still today in Los Angeles.
    </p>

    <h2>Planning ahead</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Shipping deadlines: most carriers publish last-posting dates in the first half of December. For
        international post, count back about 10 to 14 days from the 25th.
      </li>
      <li>
        Black Friday: it is tied to Thanksgiving, so it can be as early as 23 November or as late as the 29th,
        which moves the whole retail calendar from year to year.
      </li>
      <li>
        Bank holidays on a weekend: the UK gives a substitute weekday; in the US, a federal holiday on a Saturday
        is observed the Friday before, and on a Sunday the Monday after. This page does not list substitute days.
      </li>
      <li>
        Working days, not calendar days: for the number of working days before a holiday, use the business days
        calculator, which handles weekends and your own holiday list.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
